1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559751 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559751 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d4b18df5-7e14-5ba9-ad19-fa824c06c4c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559768 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559768 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559768 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559768 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559768 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559768 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559768 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559768 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559768 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f84d9338-e966-5479-8801-6e8f15658f84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559806 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559806 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73789" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559806 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559806 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-3x-2?$\n\nI. $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(3x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559806 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50aaea5b-0826-518a-8a27-2b0395848ac8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559806 #edb/ref 1074 "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(3x+2).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{2}{3}) & =3{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}+2{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-3(-\\frac{2}{3})-2 \\\\  & =3(-\\frac{8}{27})+2(\\frac{4}{9})+3(\\frac{2}{3})-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{9}+\\frac{8}{9}+2-2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ then $(3x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559822 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559822 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38040" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559822 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559822 #edb/ref 1071 "For which of the following polynomials is $(x+3)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-8x+3$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-x-3$\n\nIII. $h(x)={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x-2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559822 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "81cebe4e-1d65-5357-b789-c0fc16951ab5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559822 #edb/ref 1074 "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+3).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x+3)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=-3$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=-3$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=-3$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =2(-3)^{3}+3(-3)^{2}-8(-3)+3 \\\\  & =2(-27)+3(9)+24+3 \\\\  & =-54+27+24+3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=-3$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+3(-3)^{2}-(-3)-3 \\\\  & =-27+3(9)+3-3 \\\\  & =-27+27+3-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+3)$ is indeed a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=-3$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+2(-3)^{2}-(-3)-2 \\\\  & =-27+2(9)+3-2 \\\\  & =-27+18+3-2 \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559853 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559853 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112376" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559853 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559853 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{2}+x-6?$\n\nI. $x=3$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559853 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c04c2ae6-0944-57e5-bf4e-7441e7fccca7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559853 #edb/ref 1074 "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-3).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=3$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & ={3}^{2}+(3)-6 \\\\  & =9+3-6 \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=3$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(x-3)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(x-3)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559864 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559864 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112468" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559864 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559864 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-3x+2?$\n\nI. $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(2x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(2x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559864 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cef78ed2-9af9-556a-8a9d-05a7101331ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559864 #edb/ref 1074 "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(2x+1).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{1}{2}) & ={(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}-3(-\\frac{1}{2})+2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{3}{2}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(2x+1)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(2x+1)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559878 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559878 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73779" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559878 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559878 #edb/ref 1071 "For which of the following polynomials is $(x-2)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)={x}^{3}-4x$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{2}+4x+5$\n\nIII. $h(x)={x}^{2}-4x+4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559878 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e986899c-1ec0-569d-94ba-d6811b53879c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559878 #edb/ref 1074 "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-2).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=2$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=2$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=2$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & ={2}^{3}-4(2) \\\\  & =8-8 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x-2)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=2$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={2}^{2}+4(2)+5 \\\\  & =4+8+5 \\\\  & =17\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the factor theorem, $(x-2)$ is not a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=2$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(2) & ={2}^{2}-4(2)+4 \\\\  & =4-8+4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only .\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559896 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559896 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "05656f4c-c5e5-5079-9eb0-b865d0bab77a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559896 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186559878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559912 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559912 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c87655d-7944-5ac9-9c29-bd3ab5f7338f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559912 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186559806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559924 #